From be312021678a254dc6c374dd628a961d91f41bda Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 12:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(dsv41): direct-insert packed Engram cache rows and align
 split cache arrays

Upstream base: 19f2c20ed4d64ae22bd009ceacadd85e2a81e354

Rationale:
1. Split the 4-way set-associative disk Engram user-space row cache in _row_plan.h from an interleaved 1,096-byte struct array (where weights started at unaligned offset 33 and tags straddled 64B cache lines on 37.5% of sets across an 18 MiB footprint) into separate 64B-aligned cache_tags (512 KiB total for 64K rows, fitting in Grace L2 with one 32B tag set per half cache line), cache_clocks (16 KiB), 64B-aligned prefaulted cache_weights (16 MiB), and 8B-aligned prefaulted cache_scales (512 KiB).
2. Insert non-page-straddling packed 264B rows directly into the user-space row cache inside ple_stash_job() from the io_uring slot buffer and index two-plane/split miss staging densely by miss_idx (0..miss_count-1) rather than sparse lookup stride, eliminating the intermediate miss_weights/miss_scales bounce copy on packed single-fragment misses.
3. Deduplicate repeated misses of the same row ID within a step in ple_plan(), add a single-shard fast path when shard == 0, compact ple_fragment_t from 40B to 32B, and use insertion sort in ple_sort() for <= 32 fragments.

Quality implications: Bit-exact (torch.equal) with the existing disk Engram reader across packed and two-plane sources, cached and uncached modes, and page-boundary splits.

Tests: pytest tests/kernels/test_deepseek_v41_engram_rows.py (52 passed across 2 fresh container boots) and 2-boot run_cache_and_packed_benchmark suite.

Upstream status: Local DeepSeek-V4.1 disk Engram optimization.
